          %p
            In particular, monads as used in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming+languages"} functional programming languages
            like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haskell"} Haskell
            are really strong/enriched monads, in this way.
          %p
            In this case – and in most discussions by default – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+closed+category"} symmetric monoidal closed category
            of types is assumed to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            (“classical types”) but in contexts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubly+closed+monoidal+category"} doubly closed monoidal category
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Yet more structure on effect-monads is available in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \mapsto T(D)
            is not just an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunction"} endofunction
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphism
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monads"} idempotent monads
            this case is further discussed at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subuniverse"} reflective subuniverse
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            and maybe elsewhere.
          %h3#MonadModulesInIdeaSection Further idea: Monad modules
          %p
            Further in the practice of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BasicIdea"} above
            %em handle
            these effects.
          %blockquote
            %p
              This is particularly evident in the
              %a{:href => "#BasicIdea"} above
              running examples of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptions"} exceptions
              whose whole design purpose typically is to be handled when they arise.
          %p
            Since a “handling” of
            = succeed "-effects" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "-effect" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            should primarily consist of a program of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{E}(D) \to D
                  \\,.
          %p
            that handles effects produced by
            = succeed "-effectful" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D' \to \mathcal{E}(D)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D' \to D
          %p
            But in addition, such a handler needs to handle effects that have been “carried along”
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BindingLawInIntroduction"} (2)
            from previous computations, even along otherwise in-effectful computations
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi prog
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prog_{0,1} \;\colon\; D_{0} \to D_{1}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
