                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathcal{E}(D_1) \to D_2
                  \\,.
          %p Of such choice of effect handling, consistency demands that:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                first handling all previous effects carried along
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BindingLawInIntroduction"} (2)
                and then the newly produced effects by a given
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi prog
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prog \,\colon\, D \to \mathcal{E}(D')
                has the same result as letting
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi prog
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prog
                take care of carrying along previous effects by passing to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi prog
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prog[-]
                and then handling the resulting accumulation at once:
            %li
              %p handling the trivial effect ought to be no extra operation:
          #eq:AxiomsForEffectHandlers.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (8)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MonadicEffectHandlerConditions-221105b.jpg", :width => "560px"}/
          %p
            A data structure consisting of such assignments
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi hndl
                    %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hndl^{\mathcal{E}}
            subject to these “laws” is known as an
            %em Kleisli triple algebra
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Uustalu21Lecture2"} Uustalu (2021), Lec. 2
            %em algebra over an extension system
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "algebra+over+a+monad#ReferencesKleisliStyle"} here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            and is bijectively the same as what in traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is known as an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"}
                algebra over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            or
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              \-algebra
            or, maybe best: an
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
