                  %p
                    (cf. &amp;lbrack;
                    %a{:href => "#Uustalu21Lecture3"}> Uustalu (2021), Lect. 3, slide 9
                    \&amp;rbrack;)
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CoKleisliCompOfContextfulPrograms-230815.jpg", :width => "800px"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#DoNotation Syntactic idea: Do-notation
          %p
            Finally, to turn all this into an efficient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            one just has to declare a convenient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            for denoting
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+composition"} Kleisli composition
          %p
            One such syntax is known as “
            %em> do notation
            ” (introduced with “
            %code> {...}
            ” instead of “
            %code> do
            ” by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Launchbury93"} Launchbury 1993 §3.3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+Jones"} Mark Jones
            in the 1990s &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "Haskell#HHPW07"}> HHPW07, p. 25
            \&amp;rbrack; and adopted by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haskell"} Haskell
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://www.haskell.org/definition/from12to13.html#do"} version 1.3
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BentonHughesMoggi02"} Benton, Hughes &amp; Moggi 2002 p. 70
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Milewski19"} Milewski 2019 §20.3
          %ol
            %li
              %p successive Kleisli composition in words like “do this, do that, and return the result”,
            %li
              %p
                any intermediate bind-operation as “extracting” a
                = succeed "-datum" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                out of an
                = succeed "-datum" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℰ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}(D)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                with notation
                %code d &lt;- E
          %p
            Syntactically, do-notation is the following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+sugar"} syntactic sugar
            for combined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+composition"} Kleisli composition
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+variable"} variable binding
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %code do prog
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≡
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\equiv\;\;
                %code prog
            %li
              %p
                %code do prog1 prog2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≡
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\equiv\;\;
                %code prog1 bind (\_ -&gt; prog2)
            %li
              %p
                %code do (x &lt;- prog1) prog2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≡
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\equiv\;\;
                %code prog1 bind (\x -&gt; prog2)
          %p
            This is, first of all, a suggestive notation (in fact a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+specific+embedded+programming+language"}> domain specific embedded programming language
            ”, see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "domain+specific+embedded+programming+language#RelationToMonadicEffects"} there
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/EffectBindingViaDoNotation-230828.jpg", :width => "350px"}/
          %p
            but thereby it furthermore provides a convenient means of expressing successive Kleisli-composition simply by successivley “calling” the separate procedures, much in the style of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/imperative+programming"} imperative programming
            (which thereby is emulated/encapsulated by pure
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming"} functional programming
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/KleisliCompositeInDoNotation-230826.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
          %p
            but the notation becomes more suggestive with the rule that the “
            %code> &lt;-
            ”-symbols may notationally be suppressed for functions with trivial in- or out-put (ie. of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed "-effect," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PureEffectKleisliCompositeInDoNotation-230826.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
          %p
            This case brings out clearly how the ambient “
            %code> do
            …
            %code> return
            ”-syntax block expresses the (Kleisli-)composition of any number of
            = succeed "-effectful" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %p
            On top of that the “
            %code> &lt;-
            ”-syntax is meant to be suggestive of
            %em reading out
            a value. This is accurate imagery for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+monad"} state monad
            (and its abstraction to the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/IO-monad"} IO-monad
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi read
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mtd
                          %mi W
                          %mi State
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi read
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≡
                        %mtd
                          %mi w
                          %mo ↦
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi w
                          %mo ,
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi write
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mtd
                          %mi W
                          %mo →
                          %mi W
                          %mi State
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi write
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≡
                        %mtd
                          %mi w
                          %mo ↦
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi w
                          %mo ′
                          %mo ↦
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  read &amp;\colon&amp; W State(W)
                  \\\
                  read &amp;\equiv&amp; w \mapsto (w,w)
                  }
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\array{
                  write &amp;\colon&amp; W \to W State(\ast)
                  \\\
                  write &amp;\equiv&amp; w \mapsto (w' \mapsto w)
                  }
          %p
            any
            = succeed "-stateful" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi inc
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mi State
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi inc
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≡
                        %mtd
                          %mi n
                          %mo ↦
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  inc &amp;\colon&amp; \mathbb{N}State(\ast)
                  \\\
                  inc &amp;\equiv&amp; n \mapsto n+1
                  }
          %p may be constucted in do-notation, such as:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/StatefulNumberIncrementInDONotation-230828.jpg", :width => "560px"}/
          %p
            For a similar effect monad such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list+monad"} list monad
            the analogous
            = succeed "-code" do
              %code do
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ListReadKleisliCompositeInDoNotation-230826.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
          %p
            Here The
            = succeed "-notation" do
              %code do
            %em each step
            a number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \colon \mathbb{N}
            is “read out” from MyList and then its increment returned — but leaves linguistically implicit the idea that this operation is to be performed
            %em for all elements
            of the list and all results re-compiled into an output list.
          %p
            Indeed, in general it is misleading to think of Kleisli composition as being about “reading out” data. What Kleisli composition is really about is acting on data that has
            %em generators
            and defining a program by what it does
            = succeed "," do
              %em on generators
            %em for
            a given generating datum:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/KleisliMapsActOnGeneratingData-23-828.jpg", :width => "560px"}/
          %p
            Therefore the conceptually more accurate (if maybe less concise) program-linguistic reflection of the monadic effect-binding operation would be a “
            %code> for
            …
            %code> do
            ”-block:
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ForDoNotation-230827c.jpg", :width => "420px"}/
          %p In terms of such for-do-notation, the generic case that we started with above has the following syntactic rendering:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
